The "Hatfield" Apple-1 has sold for &#36;1,148,200 including the buyer's premium, setting a world auction record for a commercially sold Apple-1, according to <a href="https://www.bonhams.com/press_release/41771/">Bonhams</a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/apple-1-bonhams.jpg" alt="" width="1600" height="900" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1071059" /><br/>
Two bidders competed for 28 minutes before the October 14 auction closed, taking the working computer beyond its <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/02/hatfield-apple-1-auction/">&#36;500,000 to &#36;800,000 estimate</a>.<br/>
<br/>
The machine takes its name from original owner Fred Hatfield, who complained to Apple about the lack of software for the Apple-1. A letter included in the sale contains Steve Jobs' 1978 reply, offering an Apple II motherboard in exchange for the Apple-1 and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2013/05/03/working-apple-1-signed-by-woz-headed-to-auction-with-250000-to-400000-estimate/">another &#36;400</a>. Hatfield kept his computer.<br/>
<br/>
He eventually sold it privately for &#36;40,000 while it needed repairs. The buyer restored it and obtained Steve Wozniak's signature on the motherboard before it <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2013/05/25/working-apple-1-computer-sells-for-record-auction-price-of-671400/">set an auction record in 2013</a>. Bonhams lists that earlier sale as approximately &#36;686,000.<br/>
<br/>
